{"name":"Mountain Mint","hex":"#a7e0c2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain mint","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Pycnanthemum in the mint family.","Any of several plants in the genus Monardella, native to western North America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain_mint"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pycnanthemum","blocks":[{"text":"Pycnanthemum is a genus of herbaceous plants in the mint family (Lamiaceae). Species in this genus are often referred to as mountain mints and they often have a minty or thyme-like aroma when crushed. All species of Pycnanthemum are native to the United States and Canada. The center of diversity for the genus is North Carolina with 13 of the 20 species having been collected therein. Nineteen of the 20 species of Pycnanthemum occur in the Eastern US and Canada, and one disjunct species occurs in California and Oregon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Pycnanthemum_muticum_heds_and_bracts_001.JPG/330px-Pycnanthemum_muticum_heds_and_bracts_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pycnanthemum","wikidata":"Q2707696"},{"title":"Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"A mountain is an elevated portion of the surface of a planet, generally with steep sides that show significant exposed bedrock. Although there are no universally accepted definitions, a mountain is usually considered higher than a hill. By definition, mountains are often thought of as being a landform which is higher than 2,000 feet (610 m). It may either have a limited summit area or be a smaller plateau with high elevation and steep sides. A few mountains are isolated summits, but most occur in mountain ranges."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg/330px-Everest_North_Face_toward_Base_Camp_Tibet_Luca_Galuzzi_2006.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain","wikidata":"Q8502"},{"title":"Spring green","blocks":[{"text":"Spring green is a color that was traditionally considered to be on the yellow side of green, but in modern computer systems based on the RGB color model is halfway between cyan and green on the color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_green","wikidata":"Q373787"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Pycnanthemum_curvipes_%2814698304290%29.jpg/120px-Pycnanthemum_curvipes_%2814698304290%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Pycnanthemum_curvipes_%2814698304290%29.jpg/250px-Pycnanthemum_curvipes_%2814698304290%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Pycnanthemum_curvipes_%2814698304290%29.jpg/120px-Pycnanthemum_curvipes_%2814698304290%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APycnanthemum_curvipes_(14698304290).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Pycnanthemum_flexuosum_1zz.jpg/120px-Pycnanthemum_flexuosum_1zz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Pycnanthemum_flexuosum_1zz.jpg/250px-Pycnanthemum_flexuosum_1zz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Pycnanthemum_flexuosum_1